/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch28_dosing_vessel_limit / system/controlDict

  WHAT THIS CASE IS: batch27's semi-batch on the SAME fictitious reaction
  and schedule, with ONE change: the vessel connected to the reactor -- a
  DAY TANK -- holds only a quarter of reactant B, and is refilled from
  storage, batch-wise, only when it is empty (DEV.md 4c C60, second
  review).  Whatever a feed failure does, what can enter the reactor is
  bounded by what the day tank holds: the whole tank plus what has
  accumulated keeps T_cf below the limits, so the protection no longer
  depends on the dosing interlock.  A witness of the EduTool "Process
  safety" (gui/src/ui/methods/ProcessSafetyTool.tsx).

  WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT: as batch26 -- the substances are
  fictitious and every property an author-set teaching assumption; nothing
  is, or could be, validated against a measured reactor.  Narrative:
  system/flowsheetDict header.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoBatch;
description   "Semi-batch with a day tank that holds a quarter of B (fictitious A + B -> P in solvent S): the cooling fails and the whole day tank enters the reactor at once; T_cf stays low";

verbosity     2;

// ---- Time integration ----
startTime       0;        // s
endTime         18000;    // s   (5 h: four of dosing, one to finish converting)
deltaT          0.5;      // s   (the same step as batch26 and batch27)
writeInterval   50;       // s   (one trajectory row per 50 s)

//  No <t>/ time directories: the trajectory is what this case is read by,
//  and a restart from the middle of the scenario is not its question.
solutionControl
{
    write       false;
}
